# 2026-08-10 object-leak narrowing, decided from the DECLARED TYPE.
#
# ActuallyLeaks (line 68) is the CONTROL and the whole point of this fixture:
# a plain class, created, never freed. It must STILL be reported. The cheapest
# way to make a narrowing "pass" is to narrow it to nothing, and a sample that
# found 0 real leaks in 106 findings makes that failure mode very easy to reach
# by accident.
#
# Line 69, not 68: the finding anchors on the ASSIGNMENT (`P := TPlain.Create;`),
# which is where the object comes into existence, not on the `var P` declaration.
object-leak 69

# Rx (a TRegEx RECORD, line 50) and Thing (an INTERFACE, line 60) must NOT be
# reported. Neither can leak by construction -- one is never heap-allocated, the
# other is reference-counted and freeing it manually would be the actual bug --
# so the old finding was unfalsifiable: no fix could have cleared it.
#
# ProperlyFreed (line 77) must also stay silent; it was already correct and is
# here so a regression that reports EVERYTHING still fails this file.
#
# These are absence assertions on specific lines, which the .expected format
# cannot express per-line, so the counts are pinned by the positive assertion
# above being the ONLY object-leak in the file -- verified by the runner
# reporting the full actual set on mismatch.
!use-after-free
!double-free
